+## Leave-one-infill-level-out transfer (`python -m models.crack_field.transfer`, ~1 min)
+
+The model is calibrated on plain + V at four infill levels and predicts every specimen at the fifth. The table shows the stress rms on failures of the held level's U + V.
+
+The reference is `ys-net`: net stress + Walker + φ^(−n), with no notch or defect term. Its full-split held-out rms is 0.100.
+
+| held level | 100 % | 80 % | 60 % | 40 % | 20 % | mean |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| ys-net | 0.093 | 0.085 | 0.122 | 0.155 | 0.217 | 0.135 |
+| ys-lin | 0.139 | 0.077 | 0.071 | 0.115 | 0.165 | 0.113 |
+| ys-sat | 0.088 | 0.071 | 0.068 | 0.092 | 0.159 | 0.096 |
+
+What the table shows:
+- Holding out 20 % means extrapolating to d_V 1.6 mm from levels whose d_V is at most 0.84 mm. That fails for every model. The bias is −0.14 to −0.20, which is conservative.
+- With 20 % unseen, κ falls and n rises (n 0.64 against 0.40), so φ^(−n) over-weakens the 20 % level.
+- Without φ^(−n), the saturating law errs the other way at 20 % (+0.19).
+- When 100 % is held out, a0 sits on its physical bound 0.
+
+Saturation therefore helps interpolation between levels, but it is not confirmed as a law that transfers.
+
